-- title:
--   Axis Line Polynomial Ne Gives Support Eval Ne
-- statement:
--   **Two distinct degree-$d$ axis-line polynomials differ on a full interpolation support.**
--
--   Let `params` have a field model, let $x=(x_1,\ldots,x_k)$ be injective, and let $\sigma\subseteq\{1,\ldots,k\}$ have cardinality $d+1$. If $f$ and $g$ have degree at most $d$ and $f\ne g$, then some support height detects their difference:
--
--   $$
--   \exists i\in\sigma,\qquad f(x_i)\ne g(x_i).
--   $$
--
--   The support heights are distinct, so agreement at all $d+1$ of them would force the polynomial difference to have too many roots. This is the root-counting step behind vertical-line mismatch extraction.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Pasting.axisLinePolynomial_ne_gives_support_eval_ne`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
